  "query_preview": "..\n\nLittle variation in the underlying environment will exist through \nthe early week period, as conditions remain governed by a seasonably \nmoist and unstable profile held within prevailing lower amplitude \nwest-northwest upper level flow. Convective expansion underway \nupstream early this morning, tied to engagement of low level jet \nforcing into a stalled frontal zone and deeper instability \nreservoir. Observational and radar trends paired by latest CAMs \nguidance confirm expectation for some form of this activity to \narrive locally thru mid morning, given sufficient elevated \ninstability now in residence. Strongest cores will be capable of \nproducing hail and brief heavy downpours. \n\nDry conditions otherwise favored for most, if not all locations \ntoday. Tangible forcing remains elusive as greater evidence exists \nfor meaningful ascent associated with minor perturbations lodged in \nthe mean flow to hold just to the south late today and early \ntonight. There remains a lower probability outcome for the northern \nexpanse of any northern Ohio valley activity to glance across areas \ngenerally south of I-94, with this corridor most susceptible given \nproximity to the main instability gradient. Enough bulk shear exists \nto afford some convective organization should this scenario emerge, \nwith SPC highlighting this corridor for isolated strong wind/large \nhail potential. Seasonable temperatures peaking in the mid 80s.   \n\nConditional convective potential will exist both Tuesday and \nWednesday, with the forecast tenuously relying on proper allocation \nof additional shortwaves and resulting MCS activity spilling east-\nsoutheast and across the local area versus holding to the south. \nMixed signal exists each day on the possibility, with an \nunsurprisingly wide range of outcomes offered within the model \nspectrum. Outgoing forecast will continue to highlight a moderate \nprobability for rainfall, with a focus for the late day period and \ngreater potential with southward extent. Stagnant thermal profile \nmeans a continuation of highs in the lower to mid 80s. \n\nAmplified upper trough anchored over eastern Canada will lead to \nbroader confluent northwest flow developing across the great lakes \nfor the end of the work week. This will result in deep layer \nsubsidence and mid level drying as a broad 1020+ mb anticyclone \nbuilds southeast into the region. This will provide a more \ndiscernible shift in storm track south of the area. Highs will edge \nslightly lower this period - highs in the vicinity of 80 degrees.",
